@*
    The same shelf as a table, for the questions a card cannot answer.

    A card answers "what is that one". A table answers questions about the set - which of these want
    a hive mind, which come with a pack I do not own, which raise unity - because it puts the same
    field of every row under one another where they can be compared.

    Multiline rows, unlike the empire table. What is being compared here is prose: a description, a
    list of modifiers and a set of conditions are each a paragraph, and squeezing them onto one line
    would mean truncating the only thing the column is for. So the cells wrap and the rows are as
    tall as they need to be, with each one ruled off from the next.

    The name shares a column with the description rather than having one of its own. A name is two
    or three words and a description is forty, so a column each gave the shorter one a whole track
    of the table's width to leave empty and cost the longer one the room - and they are read
    together anyway, the name being what the description is about.

    The two conditions get a column each for the opposite reason: they are different questions, and
    a table is for putting the same question of every row under itself.

    The scene leads, on the pages that have one. It is the most recognisable thing about an origin
    and the thing a reader scans down the column for.

    Not EmpireTable. That one is built around an empire's columns, sorts and moves rows, and its
    cells are single-line chips - none of which is what this is. Sharing it would have meant making
    it generic in the row and then teaching it a second idea of what a cell is.
*@

<div class="sem-table-wrap">
    <table class="sem-table sem-wiki-table">
        <caption class="sem-visually-hidden">@($"Every one of the {Noun} the game defines")</caption>

        <thead>
            <tr>
                @*
                    Drawn only where something has one, which in practice means the origins. A
                    column of blanks three hundred rows deep is a column that costs width to say
                    nothing - and asking the rows rather than the page means it follows the data if
                    the game ever gives a civic a scene of its own.
                *@
                @if (_pictures)
                {
                    <th scope="col"><span class="plain">@Picture</span></th>
                }

                <th scope="col">@Header(NameColumn, "Name")</th>

                @*
                    What is true of this kind and not of the others, a column each. The headings come
                    from the rows, so a shelf that says nothing of the sort draws nothing.
                *@
                @foreach (var heading in _facts)
                {
                    <th scope="col">@Header(FactPrefix + heading, heading)</th>
                }

                @if (_effects)
                {
                    <th scope="col"><span class="plain">Effects</span></th>
                }

                @foreach (var heading in _conditions)
                {
                    <th scope="col"><span class="plain">@heading</span></th>
                }

                @*
                    Playable only where some of them are not, and Pack only where something is behind
                    one. A column answering the same thing on every row costs width to say nothing -
                    which for the ethics is both of these, since the game gates them on nothing.
                *@
                @if (_playable)
                {
                    <th scope="col">@Header(PlayableColumn, "Playable")</th>
                }

                @if (_packs)
                {
                    <th scope="col">@Header(PackColumn, "Pack")</th>
                }
            </tr>
        </thead>

        @*
            A tbody per entry rather than one for the whole table.

            Valid, and the only way to say "this entry" in CSS: an upgrade path is several rows, and
            a selector for the rows that belong to one has nothing to hold on to otherwise. Written
            as a following-sibling rule first, which lit every later step in the table at once.
        *@
        @foreach (var row in Sorted)
        {
            <tbody>
                @*
                    Pressing a row picks it out and puts it in the address, the same as pressing a
                    card does, so a reader who found the thing they came for can send somebody to it.

                    On the row rather than on a control inside it: the whole row is the entry, and
                    the chips it holds are already buttons which stop the press before it gets here.
                *@
                @*
                    A line per step of an upgrade path, and one line for everything else.

                    A column every step agrees about is written once and spans them, which is what
                    makes a path read as one entry rather than as three that happen to sit together:
                    the class and the rarity are stated once beside three tiers, and the column rules
                    run straight down through all of them.
                *@
                @foreach (var (step, first, span) in Lines(row))
                {
                <tr class="@Marks(row) @(first ? "entry" : "step")"
                    data-entry="@step.Key" @onclick="() => Press(row)">
                    @if (_pictures && first)
                    {
                        <td class="scene" rowspan="@span">
                            @if (row.Picture is { Length: > 0 } picture)
                            {
                                <img class="sem-detail-picture @Shape" src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(picture)"
                                     alt="" loading="lazy">
                            }
                            else if (row.Layers.Count > 0)
                            {
                                <div class="sem-detail-picture stacked @Shape">
                                    @foreach (var layer in row.Layers)
                                    {
                                        <img src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(layer)" alt="" loading="lazy">
                                    }
                                </div>
                            }
                        </td>
                    }

                    <th scope="row" class="name">
                        <span class="inner">
                            @*
                                Only where this shelf has pictures at all. The placeholder exists so
                                that titles line up on a shelf where some entries have an icon and
                                some do not; on a shelf where none does - the governments, the
                                personalities, the shipsets - it is an empty box beside every name,
                                a hundred and seventy times, aligning nothing with nothing.
                            *@
                            @if (Icons)
                            {
                                <GameIcon Path="@step.Icon" Alt="@step.Name" />
                            }
                            <span class="title">@step.Name</span>

                            @* The link to this one entry, at the end of the line it names. *@
                            @if (first)
                            {
                                <WikiLinkButton Link="@WikiLinks.Entry(Kind, row.Key)" Name="@row.Name" />
                            }
                        </span>

                        @*
                            Said once where the steps of a path share it, which they often do: the
                            game writes a second tier's prose as a reference to the first's, so the
                            same sentence would otherwise be printed three times over.
                        *@
                        @if (step.Description is { Length: > 0 } && (first || !SameProse(row)))
                        {
                            <span class="prose">
                                @((MarkupString)Wording.Html(step.DescriptionKey, string.Empty))
                            </span>
                        }
                    </th>

                    @foreach (var heading in _facts)
                    {
                        @if (Agreed(row, heading))
                        {
                            @if (first)
                            {
                                <td class="fact" rowspan="@span">
                                    @if (row.Fact(heading) is { } shared)
                                    {
                                        <WikiFacts Facts="@(new[] { shared })" />
                                    }
                                </td>
                            }
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <td class="fact">
                                @if (step.Fact(heading) is { } fact)
                                {
                                    <WikiFacts Facts="@(new[] { fact })" />
                                }
                            </td>
                        }
                    }

                    @if (_effects)
                    {
                        <td class="effects">
                            <EffectsList Effects="@step.Effects" />
                        </td>
                    }

                    @*
                        A column each, rather than both under one heading with the headings repeated
                        down the rows. They are different questions - what has to be true for this to
                        be drawn at all, and what has to be true for it then to be legal - and a
                        table exists to put the same question of every row under itself.

                        Empty where the game asks nothing, which is most rows for the second of them.
                        A column of "Always" three hundred times is a column saying nothing.
                    *@
                    @foreach (var heading in _conditions)
                    {
                        var stated = row.Conditions.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Heading == heading);

                        <td class="conditions" rowspan="@(first ? span : 1)"
                            hidden="@(!first && span > 1)">
                            @if (stated?.Outline is { } outline)
                            {
                                <ConditionList Node="@outline" />
                            }
                        </td>
                    }

                    @*
                        Yes or no and nothing beside it. What is behind the no - a kind of empire
                        nobody can be, or an event - is already a bullet in the column to the left,
                        and saying it twice on one row made the narrower column repeat the wider one.
                    *@
                    @if (_playable && first)
                    {
                        <td class="playable" rowspan="@span">
                            @if (row.Playable)
                            {
                                <span class="yes">Yes</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                <span class="no" title="@row.ClosedWhy">No</span>
                            }
                        </td>
                    }

                    @if (_packs && first)
                    {
                        <td class="packs" rowspan="@span">
                            <PackChips Packs="@row.Packs" />
                        </td>
                    }
                </tr>
                }

                @*
                    A second row under the first, spanning every column.

                    Thirty faces will not fit in a cell of a table that already has six columns of
                    prose, and squeezed into one they would be too small to tell apart - which is the
                    one thing a gallery is for. Given the width of the table they are the size the
                    portrait picker draws them at.
                *@
                @if (row.Gallery.Count > 0)
                {
                    <tr class="gallery @Marks(row)" data-entry="@row.Key" @onclick="() => Press(row)">
                        <td colspan="@_columns">
                            <WikiGallery Faces="@row.Gallery" Shape="@Shape" />
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        }
    </table>
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The text this shelf is written in, where a page carries its own.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The row's own key, through the page's reader. It was the key rebuilt by hand and read from
    /// the session - which ignored <c>DescriptionKey</c> entirely, so anything describing itself
    /// under another key drew a blank, and every leader trait did: their prose is in the wiki's own
    /// file and the session has never seen it.
    /// </remarks>
    [CascadingParameter(Name = "WikiText")]
    public Localizer? Reader { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whichever reader answers for this shelf.</summary>
    private Localizer Wording => Reader ?? Session.Localizer;

    /// <summary>What to draw, already narrowed and in order.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required IReadOnlyList<WikiRow> Rows { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the rows are, for the caption a screen reader meets before the table.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string Noun { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the pictures are of, for the column they are drawn in.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Picture { get; set; } = "Scene";

    /// <summary>Whether this shelf has icons, and so whether to keep room for one on every row.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Icons { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>What shape this shelf's pictures are, which decides how they are framed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Shape { get; set; } = "scene";

    /// <summary>Which shelf this is, which is half of what an entry's address is made of.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required WikiKind Kind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The key of the entry the address names, where it names one.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Chosen { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when a row is pressed, so the page can put that entry in the address.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<WikiRow> OnChoose { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What a row is marked as: out of reach, picked out, or neither.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// One method because a species row is two <c>tr</c> elements - the columns and then the gallery
    /// under them - and they have to agree, or selecting one highlights half of it.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="row">The entry.</param>
    /// <returns>The classes, which may be empty.</returns>
    private string Marks(WikiRow row) =>
        string.Join(' ', new[]
        {
            row.Playable ? null : "unreachable",

            // Any step of it, because a link may name a second tier and the whole entry is what is
            // picked out - the alternative highlights one line of a path and leaves the rest pale.
            row.Steps.Any(s => s.Key == Chosen) ? "chosen" : null,
        }.OfType<string>());

    /// <summary>
    /// The lines one entry is drawn as, each with whether it is the first and how many there are.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// One line for almost everything. An upgrade path is drawn as a line per step, and the span is
    /// what a column the steps agree about is written across.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="row">The entry.</param>
    /// <returns>Its lines.</returns>
    private static IEnumerable<(WikiRow Step, bool First, int Span)> Lines(WikiRow row)
    {
        var steps = row.Steps;

        return steps.Select((step, index) => (step, index == 0, steps.Count));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether every step of an entry says the same thing under a heading.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Compared by what the fact holds rather than by how it would be drawn, and the chips are
    /// sorted first: the game writes a trait's leader classes in whatever order it likes, so the
    /// same three classes appear as five different orderings across the file and comparing them as
    /// stated would call them different.
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="row">The entry.</param>
    /// <param name="heading">The column.</param>
    /// <returns>Whether it can be written once.</returns>
    private static bool Agreed(WikiRow row, string heading) =>
        row.Tiers.Count <= 1
        || row.Tiers.Select(t => Signature(t.Fact(heading))).Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal).Count() == 1;

    /// <summary>What a fact holds, as one comparable string.</summary>
    private static string Signature(WikiFact? fact) =>
        fact switch
        {
            null => string.Empty,
            { Text: { Length: > 0 } said } => said,
            { Tags: { Count: > 0 } tags } => string.Join('|', tags.Order(StringComparer.Ordinal)),
            { Lines: { Count: > 0 } lines } => string.Join('|', lines.Order(StringComparer.Ordinal)),
            _ => string.Join('|', fact.Chips.Select(c => c.Key).Order(StringComparer.Ordinal)),
        };

    /// <summary>Whether every step of an entry is described in the same words.</summary>
    private static bool SameProse(WikiRow row) =>
        row.Tiers.Select(t => t.DescriptionKey).Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal).Count() == 1
        || row.Tiers.Select(t => t.Description).Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal).Count() == 1;

    /// <summary>Passes a press on to the page.</summary>
    /// <param name="row">The entry pressed.</param>
    /// <returns>A task that completes when the page has dealt with it.</returns>
    private Task Press(WikiRow row) => OnChoose.InvokeAsync(row);

    /// <summary>
    /// The whole shelf, which is what the columns are decided from.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Not the narrowed rows. A column is drawn only where something fills it, and asked of the
    /// search results that made the table's shape change as the reader typed: narrowing to one
    /// playable civic took the Playable column away, and clearing the box brought it back. The
    /// shelf is what the page is about; the search only says which of it to show.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required IReadOnlyList<WikiRow> All { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether anything here carries a picture, and so whether that column is drawn.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Worked out when the rows change rather than in the markup, where it would be a pass over
    /// every row for the heading and another for each row drawn under it.
    /// </remarks>
    private bool _pictures;

    /// <summary>Whether any of them is out of reach, and so whether that column is worth drawing.</summary>
    private bool _playable;

    /// <summary>Whether any of them is behind a pack.</summary>
    private bool _packs;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether any of them does anything, which the species classes do not.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A class is what a species is made of rather than something it gains, and the game says so in
    /// its own words on every one of them: "Species Class has no effect on your Empire". So the
    /// column was forty-two blanks under a heading.
    /// </remarks>
    private bool _effects;

    /// <summary>The fact headings this shelf has, in the order its rows state them.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<string> _facts = [];

    /// <summary>And the condition headings, likewise.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<string> _conditions = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// How many columns were drawn, which is what a gallery row has to span.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Counted rather than stated, because which columns appear is decided from the rows: a span
    /// written out by hand would be right for the civics and wrong for every other shelf, and wrong
    /// silently - the row would simply stop short of the table's edge.
    /// </remarks>
    private int _columns;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which columns this shelf wants, asked of the rows rather than told by the page.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Every one of these is a column that would otherwise be blank or identical the whole way down.
    /// The ethics are the case: nothing gates them, so Playable would say Yes seventeen times and
    /// Pack nothing at all, and both would take width from the columns that do say something.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        _pictures = All.Any(r => r.Pictured);
        _playable = All.Any(r => !r.Playable);
        _packs = All.Any(r => r.Packs.Count > 0);
        _effects = All.Any(r => !r.Effects.IsEmpty);

        // Across the steps as well, so a column only a third tier fills is still drawn. An entry's
        // own facts are the union of its steps' already, but a shelf that never groups anything has
        // no steps to ask, and asking both costs one pass.
        _facts =
        [
            .. All.SelectMany(r => r.Steps).SelectMany(r => r.Facts)
                .Select(f => f.Heading)
                .Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal),
        ];

        _conditions =
        [
            .. All.SelectMany(r => r.Conditions)
                .Where(c => c.Stated)
                .Select(c => c.Heading)
                .Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal),
        ];

        _columns =
            (_pictures ? 1 : 0)
            + 1
            + _facts.Count
            + (_effects ? 1 : 0)
            + _conditions.Count
            + (_playable ? 1 : 0)
            + (_packs ? 1 : 0);
    }

    private const string NameColumn = "name";

    private const string PlayableColumn = "playable";

    private const string PackColumn = "pack";

    /// <summary>
    /// What a fact column's sort key is prefixed with, so it cannot collide with the fixed ones.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The headings come from the data, and one day a kind will have a fact called Pack. Without the
    /// prefix that column and the pack column would be one sort under two headings.
    /// </remarks>
    private const string FactPrefix = "fact:";

    /// <summary>
    /// Which column the rows are in the order of, which to begin with is the name.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The shelf hands them over in name order already, so this starts agreeing with what arrives
    /// rather than re-sorting it - and the header says so from the first draw, instead of the reader
    /// having to press a column to find out what the list is already sorted by.
    /// </remarks>
    private string _sort = NameColumn;

    private bool _descending;

    /// <summary>One sortable heading, drawn the way the empire table draws its own.</summary>
    private RenderFragment Header(string key, string label) => @<text>
        <button type="button" class="sem-sort @(_sort == key ? "sorted" : null)"
                aria-label="@($"Sort by {label}")"
                @onclick="() => SortBy(key)">
            @label
            <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">@(_sort == key ? (_descending ? "▾" : "▴") : string.Empty)</span>
        </button>
    </text>;

    /// <summary>Sorts by a column, or turns it round when it is already the one being sorted by.</summary>
    private void SortBy(string key)
    {
        if (_sort == key)
        {
            _descending = !_descending;
        }
        else
        {
            _sort = key;
            _descending = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The rows in the order the header asked for, worked out once per question.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Held against the list it was made from as well as against the sort, which is what the empire
    /// table learned: this runs on every render, and a search box that narrows the list on each
    /// keystroke would otherwise sort three hundred rows for every letter typed twice over.
    /// </remarks>
    private IReadOnlyList<WikiRow> Sorted
    {
        get
        {
            if (_ordered is { } held
                && ReferenceEquals(Rows, _orderedFor)
                && _orderedBy == _sort
                && _orderedDown == _descending)
            {
                return held;
            }

            _orderedFor = Rows;
            _orderedBy = _sort;
            _orderedDown = _descending;

            return _ordered = [.. Ordering];
        }
    }

    private IReadOnlyList<WikiRow>? _ordered;
    private IReadOnlyList<WikiRow>? _orderedFor;
    private string? _orderedBy;
    private bool _orderedDown;

    /// <summary>
    /// The rows in the order the header asked for, with the name always breaking the tie.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Two of the three sort by something with very few values - playable has two and most civics
    /// share a pack with dozens of others - so without the name settling it the same list sorted the
    /// same way would read differently depending on what it was sorted by before.
    /// </remarks>
    private IEnumerable<WikiRow> Ordering
    {
        get
        {
            if (_sort == NameColumn)
            {
                return _descending
                    ? Rows.OrderByDescending(r => r.Name, StringComparer.CurrentCulture)
                    : Rows.OrderBy(r => r.Name, StringComparer.CurrentCulture);
            }

            if (_sort.StartsWith(FactPrefix, StringComparison.Ordinal))
            {
                return Fact(_sort[FactPrefix.Length..]).ThenBy(r => r.Name, StringComparer.CurrentCulture);
            }

            // Playable first on the way up, which is the wrong way round for a boolean and the right
            // way round for a reader: the first press of a column should show the thing somebody
            // pressed it looking for.
            var ordered = _sort == PlayableColumn
                ? Key(r => r.Playable ? 0 : 1)
                : Key(r => r.Packs.Count == 0 ? 1 : 0).ThenBy(Pack, StringComparer.CurrentCulture);

            return ordered.ThenBy(r => r.Name, StringComparer.CurrentCulture);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// What a civic sorts under in the pack column, which is the pack that introduced it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The first, not the alphabetically least. A civic behind two packs almost always names the one
    /// that brought it in first, and that is the one a reader looking down the column expects to
    /// find it under.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string Pack(WikiRow row) =>
        row.Packs.Count == 0 ? string.Empty : row.Packs[0].Name;

    private IOrderedEnumerable<WikiRow> Key(Func<WikiRow, int> of) =>
        _descending ? Rows.OrderByDescending(of) : Rows.OrderBy(of);

    /// <summary>
    /// The rows in the order of one of their own facts.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// By number where the fact is one, and by word otherwise. An ethic's cost is 1, 2 or 3 and
    /// sorts the same either way today - but a fact is whatever a kind decides to state, and the
    /// first two-digit one would put 10 between 1 and 2 with nothing to say why.
    ///
    /// A row with nothing to say under the heading sorts last on the way up rather than first,
    /// because a column of blanks at the top of a list somebody sorted by it is the sort thrown
    /// away.
    /// </remarks>
    private IOrderedEnumerable<WikiRow> Fact(string heading)
    {
        if (Rows.All(r => Number(r, heading) is not null))
        {
            return Key(r => Number(r, heading)!.Value);
        }

        // Anything with nothing to say under the heading goes last whichever way the column is
        // turned. A run of blanks is not an answer, and putting it at whichever end happens to be
        // first is the sort thrown away.
        var placed = Rows.OrderBy(r => Said(r, heading).Length == 0 ? 1 : 0);

        return _descending
            ? placed.ThenByDescending(r => Said(r, heading), StringComparer.CurrentCulture)
            : placed.ThenBy(r => Said(r, heading), StringComparer.CurrentCulture);
    }

    /// <summary>What a row says under a heading, as a number where it is one.</summary>
    private static int? Number(WikiRow row, string heading) =>
        row.Fact(heading)?.Text is { Length: > 0 } text
        && int.TryParse(text, System.Globalization.NumberStyles.Integer, System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, out var value)
            ? value
            : null;

    /// <summary>And as words, which for a fact that names things is the names run together.</summary>
    private static string Said(WikiRow row, string heading) =>
        row.Fact(heading) switch
        {
            { Text: { Length: > 0 } text } => text,
            { Tags: { Count: > 0 } tags } => string.Join(", ", tags),
            { Lines: { Count: > 0 } lines } => string.Join(" ", lines),
            { Chips: { Count: > 0 } chips } => string.Join(", ", chips.Select(c => c.Name)),
            _ => string.Empty,
        };
}
